WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.845
A conditional conclusion claims that one condition would be enough for another.

2
00:00:05.145 --> 00:00:09.476
It remains hypothetical until a fact supplies its sufficient condition.

3
00:00:09.776 --> 00:00:11.803
Every archived sample is labelled.

4
00:00:12.103 --> 00:00:14.108
Every labelled sample is retained.

5
00:00:14.408 --> 00:00:16.925
Therefore every archived sample is retained.

6
00:00:17.225 --> 00:00:19.252
Imagine a sample that is archived.

7
00:00:19.552 --> 00:00:22.283
Labelling follows, and then retention follows.

8
00:00:22.583 --> 00:00:27.788
That establishes the conclusion without establishing that any archived sample exists.

9
00:00:28.088 --> 00:00:32.547
An answer saying an archived sample exists would add a different kind of claim.

10
00:00:32.847 --> 00:00:40.015
A retained but unarchived sample would not refute the stated conditional; an archived unretained sample would.

11
00:00:40.315 --> 00:00:43.664
Conditional conclusions occur in several LSAT tasks.

12
00:00:43.964 --> 00:00:50.513
Separating the claim’s form from a case fact makes both proof and counterexample construction more precise.

13
00:00:50.813 --> 00:00:53.075
Ask what the premises force if it is true.

14
00:00:53.375 --> 00:00:56.276
Do not count the conclusion itself among the premises.

15
00:00:56.576 --> 00:00:59.499
Finish by saying the answer as a complete sentence.

16
00:00:59.799 --> 00:01:04.855
Ask what it asserts, which group it covers and whether each part was actually established.

17
00:01:05.155 --> 00:01:10.488
Fresh mixed practice should require choosing the reasoning, not repeating the lesson’s example.
